# How long a browser may keep what: it may keep everything, and has to ask each time it uses
# a file whether it is still the same ("no-cache"; the answer is a short "unchanged" when it
# is). Without saying so, Cloudflare lets browsers keep any answer for four hours, a setting
# of the whole domain, which is not Bearing's to change.
#
# There was a rule here that had everything under /assets/ kept for a year, since those
# files carry a fingerprint of their contents in their names. It broke the site on
# 2026-11-17. For the seconds a new version takes to spread, a browser can ask one server
# for a file that only the newer server has, and a site of this kind answers every unknown
# address with its page. The rule then had that wrong answer kept for a year, by Cloudflare
# and by the browser, under the name of a script. The same went for the chess pieces and
# the engine. The code now lives under /static/, the pieces under /chessmen/ and the engine
# under /stockfish/, so that nothing kept under the old addresses can be used again. Do not
# put a long-lived rule on any of them.

# The app is not meant to be shown inside another site's page, and asks for nothing of the
# device it runs on.
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
